<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Widukind of Corvey</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 925–after 973</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/widukind">Widukind</a> of Corvey was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> monk of the abbey of Corvey and author of the Deeds of the Saxons (Res gestae Saxonicae), written in the late 960s and early 970s. It is a chief source for the Ottonian kings <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-fowler">Henry the Fowler</a> and Otto I, including Otto's victory at the Lechfeld in 955, and for the eastern frontier under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gero-the-great">Margrave Gero</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/widukind-of-corvey.jpg" alt="Widukind of Corvey" width="151" height="189" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:WIDUKIND.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The kings endowed monasteries and convents such as Quedlinburg, Gandersheim and Corvey, which became centres of learning: the canoness Hrotsvitha of Gandersheim wrote Latin plays and poems in the tenth century, and the monk <b>Widukind of Corvey</b> wrote a history of the Saxons.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-saxony">Duchy of Saxony · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Harald’s conversion, usually dated to about 965 and linked by the chronicler <b>Widukind of Corvey</b> to the missionary Poppo, tied the kingdom to the German church.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-denmark">Kingdom of Denmark · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Widukind of Corvey</b> reports that he killed some thirty Slavic chiefs at a banquet.</blockquote><a href="/state/marca-geronis">Marca Geronis</a></li><li><blockquote>Corvey sent Ansgar on his mission to Denmark and Sweden, and its school produced <b>Widukind of Corvey</b>, whose Deeds of the Saxons is a chief source for the Ottonian kings.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-abbey-of-corvey">Prince-Abbey of Corvey</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. Died in Princely Abbey of Corvey. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51,10&amp;year=956" aria-label="Germany on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4777 395 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Widukind of Corvey"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5227 665h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5227 665h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51,10&amp;year=956">What was here in 956 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
